= Conjecture — Technical =

== Formal status ==
Let T be a mathematical theory and φ a statement.

A conjecture is often a statement for which neither a proof nor a disproof is known:

<pre>
T ⊬ φ
T ⊬ ¬φ
</pre>

from the current state of mathematical knowledge.

This epistemic status is distinct from formal independence, which requires proof that neither φ nor ¬φ is derivable from T.

== Evidence ==
Computational verification, asymptotic evidence, probabilistic heuristics, analogy, and partial cases can raise confidence without changing proof status.

== Counterexamples ==
For universal statements:

<pre>
∀x P(x)
</pre>

one instance with ¬P(x) refutes the conjecture.

== Independence ==
Some conjecture-like statements can later be shown independent of a chosen axiom system.

That does not make them meaningless; it changes the question to stronger axioms or alternative frameworks.

